18.55. socket_get_var(name, socket_name=’socket_0’) Reads an integer from the server Sends the message "GET <name>\n" through the socket, expects the response "<name> <int>\n" within 2 seconds. Returns 0 after timeout Parameters name: Variable name (string) socket_name: Name of socket (string) Return Value PolyScope X 126 Script Directory 18. Module interfaces Copyright © 2009–2026 by Universal Robots A/S. All rights reserved. an integer from the server (int), 0 is the timeout value Example command: x_pos = socket_get_var("POS_X") Sends: GET POS_X\n to socket_0, and expects response within 2s • Example Parameters: • name = POS_X -> name of variable • socket_name = default: socket_0

**Source 15 (documentation):**
Socket: Socket communication can be used within secondary programs, but this is limited to executing simple non-blocking commands like single socket_send. Slow remote server or attempt to execute multiple socket commands will cause "Runtime too much behind", or prevent controller from executing real-time tasks. Socket commands should be avoided in secondary programs. XMLRPC: Like socket, XMLRPC calls can be used in secondary programs although they need to be executed very quickly by remote server. XMLRPC calls should be avoided in secondary programs. Sleep: Sleep, similar to motion commands is a blocking call by nature. It requires multiple real-time cycles to complete. Using Sleep will cause an error of "Runtime is too much behind" Threads: Threads in secondary programs are not supported. Thread code execution is not guaranteed, and leads to undefined behaviour. PolyScope X 20 Script Directory
